Timeline
--------
2026-09-13, hydra, dev block 1 (router :9010, apps :9011-9014, mock :9015),
node 24.14.1, jq 1.8.1.

1. Read #14. Chose option 1 (the estate serves its own route table) with
   option 3 (refuse) as the fallback when it cannot, rather than option 3
   alone: a refusal on sha mismatch still leaves the CI case -- runner tree
   and deployed build routinely differ there by design -- with no way to run
   the gate at all. Option 1 gives that case a correct oracle; option 3 is
   what happens when option 1 is unavailable.

2. FAILED, first cut of gates/oracle.sh: it accepted any JSON reply with a
   non-empty .routes array. Against a router that predates the manifest,
   /__estate.json falls through to core, whose 404 body is
   {app,path,found,block,sha,routes:[...]} -- a routes array, of strings,
   for one app. The gate built an "8 apps" oracle out of a 5-app estate and
   died in jq instead of refusing. Trusting the shape of a reply without
   checking who sent it is the same mistake one level down. Now requires
   served_by=="router" and an array of app objects, and lint-app.mjs reserves
   /__ so no app can own that path honestly.

3. FAILED, ORACLE_MODE=tree was checked only after the manifest fetch, so the
   escape hatch silently upgraded itself to the estate's oracle and could not
   reproduce the behaviour it exists to reproduce. Moved to resolution step 0.

4. FAILED, experiments/014 left its stub listening on :9016: the EXIT trap
   removed the temp dir before reading the pid file out of it, so the next run
   died EADDRINUSE. Kill first, then remove.

5. Corrected `[ "$ORACLE_FROM" = estate ] && seen=...` to an if block. As an
   AND-list with no || tail under set -e it is a portability trap, and
   gates/e2e.sh runs under dash in CI and sh on hydra.

